# PocketHaven Asset Naming Conventions

Rules for naming decoration `id`s and `displayName`s. Read this before authoring any new `DecorationData` asset.

The one rule that matters most: **an `id` is permanent.** Once an item ships and players have save data referencing it, the `id` can never change without breaking their rooms. Choose it carefully and treat it as write-once.

## Core principle

- **`id`** is a permanent machine key. Lowercase, namespaced, never changes, encodes only what is invariant about the item.
- **`displayName`** is a mutable human label, the player-facing name. Can be edited, rethemed, or localized at any time.
- The two never depend on each other. You can rewrite a `displayName` freely without touching anything else.

## The id format

```
pack.item-slug
```

Examples:

| id | meaning |
|----|---------|
| `base.oak-stool` | Oak stool, free starter set |
| `base.round-rug` | Round rug, free starter set |
| `thornwood.thorn-armchair` | Thorn armchair, Thornwood pack |
| `seelie.fae-lantern` | Fae lantern, Seelie pack |
| `mossgrove.toadstool-lamp` | Toadstool lamp, Mossgrove pack |

### id rules

- All lowercase. No spaces, no capitals, no special characters.
- A dot separates the pack from the item. Hyphens separate words inside the slug.
- **Write-once.** Never rename an id after the item ships. To replace an item, ship a new id, do not edit the old one.
- Must be unique across the entire catalog, forever. The pack prefix guarantees this.

## What must never go in an id

Anything that can change. Putting mutable info in an id means the id eventually lies.

- No price.
- No free-versus-paid flag. That lives in `isPremium`.
- No category. That lives in `category`.
- No color, pattern, or size. Those are variant indices, see below.

An id encodes only the two things that are truly permanent: **which pack it came from** and **which item it is.**

## packId conventions

- Reserve **`base`** for the free starter set. Swap for `core` if you prefer, but pick one and stay with it.
- Keep packIds short, lowercase, and meaningful: `thornwood`, `seelie`, `mossgrove`.
- A packId, once used, is reserved forever. Log every one in the Pack Registry below.
- For premium items, set `iapBundleId` to the packId. An IAP purchase then unlocks every item whose id starts with that pack prefix.

## Slug conventions

- Singular nouns: `oak-stool`, not `oak-stools`.
- Descriptive, optionally style-qualified: `tall-bookshelf`, `round-rug`, `thorn-armchair`.
- Lowercase, hyphen-separated, no abbreviations unless they are obvious.
- Be consistent. If you use `lamp` once, do not use `light` for the same kind of object elsewhere.

## Variants are not separate items

Recolors and patterns are stored as an index on the placed instance, not as new catalog entries.

- A recolor is a `colorIndex` on the placed item. `colorIndex = 0` is the base color.
- A pattern (IAP) will be a `patternIndex`, added later.
- A chair offered in 8 colors is **one** `id` with a color index, not 8 ids.

This keeps the catalog from ballooning into thousands of near-duplicate entries.

## displayName conventions

- Title Case, friendly, in the fae aesthetic: "Oak Stool", "Thornwood Armchair", "Fae Lantern".
- Fully decoupled from the id. Rename or retheme any time.
- Localization-ready: because the id is stable, you can later swap `displayName` for a localization key keyed off the id, without touching any save data. Matters for global and Switch releases.

## Store SKU mapping

- The internal packId is your stable reference.
- Each store (App Store, Google Play, Steam) wants its own product id format.
- Map packId to each store's SKU in ONE place. Do not scatter store ids through the codebase.

## Code rule

In code, reference the `DecorationData` asset directly, never the id string. The secret-room trigger, for example, points at a `DecorationData` object, not the text `"base.oak-stool"`. The only place id strings should ever appear is save data, which leaves almost nothing to typo.

## Adding a new item: checklist

1. Pick the packId. New pack? Add it to the Pack Registry first.
2. Write the id as `pack.item-slug`: lowercase, singular, hyphenated.
3. Confirm the slug is consistent with existing items (same words for the same objects).
4. Set `displayName` in friendly Title Case.
5. Set `category`, `isPremium`, and `iapBundleId` (packId for premium, empty for base).
6. Leave `footprint` and `supportsRecolor` at sensible defaults.
7. Never touch the id again after the item ships.

